WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:10.890
Hi! A big bottle of water and a small cup: which holds more? Today we'll learn about volume and measure liquids in litres.

2
00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:16.218
Volume of liquid is how much water, milk or juice a container holds.

3
00:00:16.368 --> 00:00:21.426
We measure it in litres, written with a small l, so 3 litres is 3 ℓ.

4
00:00:21.576 --> 00:00:25.074
A big carton of milk holds about 1 litre.

5
00:00:25.224 --> 00:00:31.602
Look at the scale on the jug. The number at the water level tells the litres.

6
00:00:31.752 --> 00:00:36.522
5 litres is more than 3 litres, and 3 is more than 2.

7
00:00:36.672 --> 00:00:42.618
Pour two pails together and add: 3 litres plus 5 litres is 8 litres.

8
00:00:42.768 --> 00:00:48.786
Pour some out and subtract: 10 litres minus 4 litres leaves 6 litres.

9
00:00:48.936 --> 00:00:55.050
Share 12 litres equally into 3 bottles: 4 litres each.

10
00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:59.658
Watch out! A taller bottle doesn't always hold more!

11
00:00:59.808 --> 00:01:03.930
A tall thin bottle may hold less than a short wide one.

12
00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:08.946
Pour the water out and read the scale to find out.

13
00:01:09.096 --> 00:01:19.002
Your turn! The water level is at 4. How many litres of water? Pause and think.

14
00:01:19.152 --> 00:01:25.146
The level is at 4, so 4 litres. Did you get it right?

15
00:01:25.296 --> 00:01:34.554
Another one! Which is more, 6 litres or 9 litres? Pause and think.

16
00:01:34.704 --> 00:01:41.178
9 is more than 6, so 9 litres is more. Did you get it right?

17
00:01:41.328 --> 00:01:52.266
Here's another! One pail has 4 litres and another has 7. How much altogether? Pause and think.

18
00:01:52.416 --> 00:01:58.890
4 plus 7 is 11, so 11 litres. Did you get it right?

19
00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:10.410
Try one more! A tank has 15 litres and 6 litres are used. How much is left? Pause and think.

20
00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:17.586
15 minus 6 is 9, so 9 litres are left. Did you get it right?

21
00:02:17.736 --> 00:02:28.530
Try one more! Each bottle holds 2 litres. How many litres in 5 bottles? Pause and think.

22
00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:34.674
5 twos are 10, so 10 litres. Did you get it right?

23
00:02:34.824 --> 00:02:46.098
Last one! 20 litres are shared equally into 4 pails. How many litres in each? Pause and think.

24
00:02:46.248 --> 00:02:53.274
20 divided by 4 is 5, so 5 litres each. Did you get it right?

25
00:02:53.424 --> 00:03:01.602
Remember: we measure liquids in litres, written ℓ. Read the scale; add when we pour together, subtract when we pour out.

26
00:03:01.752 --> 00:03:07.650
Look at a drink bottle at home. How many litres does it say?

